//Class
A class is a user-defined data type that groups data and functions together.
It groups data (variables) and functions that belong together.
For example:
        
class Student {
public:
    string name;
    int age;

    void study() {
        cout << "Student is studying";
    }
};

Here Student is a class.

It contains:

Student -> Class name
│
├── name       → data/member variable
├── age        → data/member variable
│
└── study()    → member function

So the class describes what a Student object has and what it can do.

Why do we use classes?

Without a class, you might have:

string name;
int age;

void study() {
    ...
}

These things are separate.

With a class, we group related things:

class Student {
public:
    string name;
    int age;

    void study() {
        ...
    }
};

Now everything related to a Student is grouped together.